        {
          "path": "_m/9/m/g/v/santa-catalina-island-california-wp/buffalo-corral-remanents-31892.jpg",
          "filename": "Buffalo Corral Remanents (31892034042).jpg",
          "caption": "Buffalo (correctly referred to as bison) corral posts dot the Trans-Catalina Trail along with healthy clusters of cacti and other native plants.\nCrows perch atop the posts and wedge prickly pears into the wood, allowing them to peck the fruit inside.\nOn the previous day, I was charged by Bison and was only able to extract myself from the situation after five repeats of the same charge. Keep your distance! Bison are wild animals. I learned my lesson; do not underestimate how much room you need to leave between you and the herd.\n\nIMG_20161225_092155160",
          "alt": "Buffalo (correctly referred to as bison) corral posts dot the Trans-Catalina Trail along with healthy clusters of cacti and other native plants.\nCrows perch atop the posts and wedge prickly pears into the wood, allowing them to peck the fruit inside.\nOn the previous day, I was charged by Bison and was only able to extract myself from the situation after five repeats of the same charge. Keep your distance! Bison are wild animals. I learned my lesson; do not underestimate how much room you need to leave between you and the herd.\n\nIMG_20161225_092155160",
          "width": 4160,
          "height": 2340,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
          "artist": "Chris Hunkeler from Carlsbad, California, USA",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buffalo_Corral_Remanents_(31892034042).jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "santa-catalina-island-california-wp",
          "sourceGeohash4": "9mgv",
          "accessedAt": "2026-02-21T20:20:08.649Z",
          "smallPath": "_m/9/m/g/v/santa-catalina-island-california-wp/buffalo-corral-remanents-31892-small.webp",
          "mediumPath": "_m/9/m/g/v/santa-catalina-island-california-wp/buffalo-corral-remanents-31892-medium.webp"
        },
        {
          "path": "_m/9/m/g/v/santa-catalina-island-california-wp/catalina-trail-western-terminu.jpg",
          "filename": "Catalina Trail Western Terminus (31922473241).jpg",
          "caption": "This is the western terminus of the Trans-Catalina Trail. \nThe road ends abruptly at a cliff with a sign pointing to the beach. The sign stands precariously in a clump of clay soil about to tumble over the edge.\nIt was not immediately clear how to get down to the beach but a hike backup the road and along a cow trail led to an impenetrable thicket. Instead, one needs to put ones life at risk by passing under a crumbling cliff face on unstable rocks and then proceed along the cliff edge until you finally make it down to this location on the beach. \nThis was my lunch spot. A recreational fishing boat is seen off shore and the beach spreads out in the opposite direction as seen in the next photo.\n\nIMG_20161226_145529536",
          "alt": "This is the western terminus of the Trans-Catalina Trail. \nThe road ends abruptly at a cliff with a sign pointing to the beach. The sign stands precariously in a clump of clay soil about to tumble over the edge.\nIt was not immediately clear how to get down to the beach but a hike backup the road and along a cow trail led to an impenetrable thicket. Instead, one needs to put ones life at risk by passing under a crumbling cliff face on unstable rocks and then proceed along the cliff edge until you finally make it down to this location on the beach. \nThis was my lunch spot. A recreational fishing boat is seen off shore and the beach spreads out in the opposite direction as seen in the next photo.\n\nIMG_20161226_145529536",
          "width": 4160,
          "height": 2340,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
          "artist": "Chris Hunkeler from Carlsbad, California, USA",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catalina_Trail_Western_Terminus_(31922473241).jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "santa-catalina-island-california-wp",
          "sourceGeohash4": "9mgv",
          "accessedAt": "2026-02-21T20:20:08.649Z",
